diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — pc1 (10.5.0.1, Campus 2) unable to reach the 172.16.30.x (VLAN30) subnet on Campus 1 — cannot be reproduced in the live lab and leaves no evidence of a past fault.

A full three-phase diagnostic sweep was performed. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL or firewall rules on any device, no duplicate MAC addresses, no static ARP poisoning, and no addressing or gateway anomalies across all 15 hosts. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot + per-router routing tables) confirmed that all nine OSPF routers hold Full adjacencies and carry 18 routes each; 172.16.30.0/24 is present in every router's FIB at exactly the same metric and ECMP next-hops as the working 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 prefixes. No traffic-shaping qdisc rules exist on any interface. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion on any device.

A direct ICMP probe from pc1 to 172.16.30.12 returned 4/4 packets with 0% loss and normal RTT, confirming end-to-end reachability between the reported source and destination subnet right now. The complete absence of any flap script, ACL rule, TC rule, routing asymmetry, or link anomaly that could explain an intermittent failure means the user's claim is unconfirmed by any current or historical mechanism.

Root cause: no fault is present; the network is fully healthy between Campus 2 (10.5.0.x) and the 172.16.30.x subnet.

Fix: no corrective action required; re-test from the user's workstation and verify the client-side host configuration (correct default gateway, no local firewall rules).
=== END ===